WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.170
On an island in the middle of the Indian Ocean, a watch officer took the distant rumble for a ship's guns and wrote in his log,

2
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:16.260
"probably heavy gunfire." The sound had come from four thousand eight hundred kilometers away, from a mountain that no longer existed.

3
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.390
That morning the earth made the loudest sound in its recorded history.

4
00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:33.840
The Sunda Strait is a narrow waterway that separates the islands of Java and Sumatra.

5
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:46.290
It is one of the busiest sea passages in the world; at the close of the nineteenth century, steamships out of Europe, sailing vessels loaded with spices, and the gunboats of the Dutch colonial navy all passed through it.

6
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:53.140
In the middle of the strait, between those two great islands, lay a small island that nobody paid much attention to.

7
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:59.510
Roughly nine kilometres long, five kilometres wide, covered from head to foot in tropical forest.

8
00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.720
Its name was Krakatoa.

9
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:09.850
The island was not really an island; it was a single body formed where three separate volcanic cones had merged above the surface of the sea.

10
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:16.380
At its northern end rose Perbuwatan, low and broad, only one hundred and twenty-two metres above the water.

11
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.670
In the middle stood Danan, four hundred and forty-five metres.

12
00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:29.760
And in the south stood the master of the island: Rakata, a steep cone of eight hundred and thirteen metres, its summit more often than not inside the clouds.

13
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.730
To sailors, Rakata was a landmark, not a threat.

14
00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:38.820
Passing through the strait they would look at it, correct their course by it, and then forget it.

15
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.350
They had reasons to forget.

16
00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:47.720
Krakatoa's last great rage went back to the sixteen-eighties, and even the memory of that event had grown blurred.

17
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.410
For two centuries the mountain had slept.

18
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:55.660
Its forest had returned, birds had settled, fishermen rested along its shore.

19
00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:03.150
There was no permanent settlement on the island, though people came over from Sumatra from time to time to cut timber from its woods.

20
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:15.280
Geology was still a young science; the conceptual framework that would say a volcano falling silent for two hundred years does not mean it has died, but may instead mean that suppressed pressure is accumulating,

21
00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.250
had not yet fully taken hold in those years.

22
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.220
The first sign came on the twentieth of May, eighteen eighty-three.

23
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:28.470
Perbuwatan, the lowest and northernmost cone of the island, woke up with a roar.

24
00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:35.800
In Batavia, today's Jakarta, windows rattled; the city was more than one hundred and sixty kilometres away.

25
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.810
Ash and steam climbed into the sky.

26
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:47.740
The officers of a German warship named the Elisabeth, passing through the strait at the time, recorded that the column reached a height of about eleven kilometres.

27
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:52.190
That meant a serious eruption, but it did not mean a catastrophe.

28
00:02:52.190 --> 00:03:02.880
The Indonesian archipelago is full of volcanoes; everyone who lived in the region was used to the occasional fall of ash, to tremors in the ground, to columns of smoke in the sky.

29
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.130
What happened next is a scene that, looking back today, makes the skin crawl.

30
00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.300
Krakatoa turned into a tourist attraction.

31
00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:17.830
Dutch merchants and officials in Batavia hired boats at the weekend and organised excursions to the island.

32
00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:25.640
They walked on the hot ash, climbed right up to the rim of the crater and looked down into it, collected scorched branches as souvenirs.

33
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.690
Photographs were taken.

34
00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:34.060
One engineer described throwing his hat into the air inside the column of steam rising from the crater's mouth.

35
00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.550
The island looked picturesque rather than dangerous.

36
00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.720
June came, and Danan woke as well.

37
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.730
Now two cones were erupting at once.

38
00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:54.420
In July, ships passing through the strait reported floating layers of pumice on the surface of the water; in some places that layer had thickened so much that vessels struggled to force a path through it.

39
00:03:54.420 --> 00:04:00.470
In early August, a Dutch engineer who landed on the island counted eleven separate eruption points.

40
00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:08.920
The surface of the island was no longer green; it was covered in a grey blanket of ash and stone dust, and the trees had been reduced to charcoal.

41
00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.930
Even so, nobody evacuated the region.

42
00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:16.540
Nobody so much as considered it, because there was no way of knowing what was coming.

43
00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:26.110
Volcano monitoring did not exist in that era: there was no seismograph network, no gas measurement, no instrument that could show a magma chamber swelling.

44
00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:31.200
The only data available was what people saw with their eyes and heard with their ears.

45
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.930
And what could be seen was a mountain that had been grumbling continuously but within limits for three months.

46
00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:42.340
As long as the grumbling continued, the human mind was normalising it.

47
00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:48.390
By the twenty-sixth of August, tens of thousands of people were living on the two shores of the Sunda Strait.

48
00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:56.520
On the Java side there was Anyer, the most important port town on the strait, the terminus of the Dutch postal and telegraph line.

49
00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:02.090
A little to the north lay Merak, a workers' town that lived off its stone quarries.

50
00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:10.380
On the Sumatra side there was Teluk Betung, the administrative and commercial centre of the Lampung region, a crowded port built at the head of a bay.

51
00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:17.230
Between them, only thirteen kilometres from Krakatoa, sat the island of Sebesi, home to three thousand people.

52
00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:22.800
All of them were living an ordinary summer's day on top of a pressure swelling at the bottom of the sea.

53
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.690
On Sunday the twenty-sixth of August, at around one in the afternoon, the sound changed.

54
00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:35.860
It was no longer a grumble; it was a series of hard, dry detonations, one after another.

55
00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:43.990
Towards two o'clock the black cloud that appeared above Krakatoa climbed past a height of twenty-five kilometres and spread open like an umbrella.

56
00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:46.520
The strait went dark.

57
00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:56.250
Towards evening, ships some twenty kilometres away reported pumice and hot ash raining onto their decks; so hot that the sailors could not touch it without burning their hands.

58
00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:04.380
That night an English sailing ship named the Charles Bal, passing through the strait, left behind a testimony that entered the historical record.

59
00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:11.070
Her captain and crew stayed some fifteen kilometres from the island for hours and recorded what was happening hour by hour.

60
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:22.880
By their account the sky was like ink, yet it kept lighting up: lightning flickering inside the clouds, blue lights appearing at the tips of the masts, sparks running across the surface of the sea.

61
00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.490
The sound was violent enough to make it impossible for the crew to hear one another.

62
00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:33.220
Under the weight of the ash they were forced to clear the deck at regular intervals.

63
00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.390
The real event came the following morning.

64
00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:44.840
On the morning of Monday the twenty-seventh of August, at half past five, the first great explosion came; its source was Perbuwatan.

65
00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:49.130
At six forty-four came the second, this time from Danan.

66
00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:51.820
Then a short silence.

67
00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:58.670
And at two minutes past ten in the morning, the most powerful sound recorded in human history was produced.

68
00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:03.760
What happened in that moment was not a simple explosion but a structural collapse.

69
00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:10.130
The magma chamber beneath the island had emptied, and the mountain above it fell into that void under its own weight.

70
00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:18.260
A mass of rock hundreds of metres thick settled, all at once, into seawater that was in contact with magma hotter than a thousand degrees.

71
00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:22.390
The energy released destroyed more than seventy per cent of the island.

72
00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:25.720
Perbuwatan and Danan disappeared entirely.

73
00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:32.890
Rakata was split in two; its northern half sank into the sea, its southern half remained standing as a sheer cliff.

74
00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:39.260
What was left behind was an enormous caldera pit descending two hundred and fifty metres below the seafloor.

75
00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:44.510
Roughly twenty cubic kilometres of rock, ash and pumice were hurled into the atmosphere.

76
00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:49.280
The ash column reached a height of eighty kilometres, far beyond the stratosphere.

77
00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.530
The sound wave spread out of the strait, across the ocean, in every direction.

78
00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:58.340
In western Java, people felt their eardrums burst.

79
00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:04.070
On ships sixty kilometres from Krakatoa, sailors' eardrums genuinely ruptured.

80
00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:10.120
The pressure gauge at the gasworks in Batavia recorded a jump in which the needle flew clean off the paper.

81
00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:21.130
Even one hundred and sixty kilometres from the source, the sound pressure exceeded one hundred and seventy decibels according to modern calculations; a value far beyond standing next to a jet engine.

82
00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:24.460
But the sound did not stop.

83
00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:26.990
It kept spreading.

84
00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:36.880
In the west of Australia, in Perth, three thousand one hundred and ten kilometres away, people reported hearing heavy artillery fire in the distance during the morning hours.

85
00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:41.970
In the centre of the continent, at Alice Springs, the same thing was heard.

86
00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:51.060
And the furthest range of the sound was at the other end of the Indian Ocean, on the island of Rodrigues near Mauritius: four thousand eight hundred kilometres from Krakatoa.

87
00:08:51.060 --> 00:09:00.950
The island's chief of police recorded that he had heard heavy rumbling from the east that morning, like the sound of cannon, and had written a report supposing it came from a naval battle.

88
00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:08.120
It was only weeks later that it became clear that the sound, having crossed a quarter of the world, belonged to a volcano.

89
00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:12.730
This is the known limit of the range a sound can travel through the air.

90
00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:19.740
Beyond four thousand kilometres the sound had turned into a pressure wave of frequencies too low for the human ear to catch.

91
00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:22.110
But it had not disappeared.

92
00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:26.400
At ten forty-one came the fourth and last great explosion.

93
00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:31.650
After that, what remained was an invisible wave circling the entire atmosphere.

94
00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:38.820
The pressure wave Krakatoa produced went around the world three and a half times; for five days it travelled the planet.

95
00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:51.750
From London to Buenos Aires, from Vienna to Calcutta, the barographs at meteorological stations, the instruments that draw air pressure onto paper, recorded the same small jump exactly seven times: with every pass,

96
00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.960
the wave moved the needle.

97
00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:58.410
Not one of those measuring stations knew what it had recorded that day.

98
00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.100
The papers were archived.

99
00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.950
Their meaning would be deciphered later.

100
00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:06.480
Those who heard the sound survived.

101
00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.290
The ones who died were those who never had the time to hear it.

102
00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:15.540
The collapse of the mountain into the sea pushed the water out as a single enormous mass.

103
00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:23.990
The Sunda Strait is narrow and shallow; the wave could not spread and dissipate the way it would in open ocean, and as it compressed towards the shore it rose.

104
00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:30.520
A little after ten in the morning, a wall of water more than thirty metres high struck both sides of the strait.

105
00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:36.890
The mark measured at Merak was above forty metres; the water had climbed as high as the houses on the hilltop.

106
00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:39.900
Anyer disappeared first.

107
00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:48.510
It was the tidiest, most European town on the strait; it had a stone quay, a warehouse, a hotel, a telegraph office.

108
00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:52.800
After the wave passed, what remained was flattened ground and mud.

109
00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.130
The lighthouse on the shore was torn out of its foundation.

110
00:10:56.130 --> 00:11:02.820
In those same hours the water rode over the island of Sebesi; the island was low and the wave went straight across it.

111
00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.270
Of the three thousand people living there, not a single one survived.

112
00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:13.640
On the Sumatra side, Teluk Betung had the worst geography of all, sitting at the very head of its bay.

113
00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:17.610
The narrow bay gathered the wave like a funnel and lifted it.

114
00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:24.140
The town was wiped out along with its harbour, its market, its Dutch administrative building and its neighbourhoods.

115
00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:35.310
The most concrete evidence of the wave's height there is where a ship ended up: the Dutch gunboat Berouw, at anchor in the harbour, was lifted by the water, carried two and a half kilometres inland from the shoreline,

116
00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:40.080
and left about nine metres above sea level on the bank of a riverbed.

117
00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.330
The ship stood there for years, inside the forest, its hull almost intact.

118
00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:49.140
Of her twenty-eight crew, none survived.

119
00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:54.070
In those same waters, in those same hours, one ship was saved.

120
00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:58.200
The Dutch steamer Loudon lay at anchor off Teluk Betung.

121
00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:07.450
When her captain saw a wall of water approaching on the horizon, he cut the anchor chain, opened the engines to full, and turned her bow directly into the wave.

122
00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:17.500
This is a manoeuvre known in seamanship but requiring nerve to execute: to be caught side-on by a wave is to capsize, while entering it at a right angle gives you a chance to climb.

123
00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:23.550
The steamer climbed that mountain of water, passed over its back, and came down the other side.

124
00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:28.800
Those watching from the deck saw the town they had left behind vanish within a few minutes.

125
00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:37.730
On the two sides of the strait, a total of one hundred and sixty-five towns and villages were completely erased, and hundreds more were badly damaged.

126
00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:45.060
The count made afterwards by the Dutch colonial administration recorded the death toll as thirty-six thousand four hundred and seventeen.

127
00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:55.910
Of those, twenty-one thousand five hundred and sixty-five were in the Banten region on the Java side, and twelve thousand four hundred and sixty-six in Lampung on the Sumatra side.

128
00:12:55.910 --> 00:13:06.440
That figure is the official count and is in all likelihood incomplete: fishing communities along the coast, unregistered villages, small boats out at sea could not be fully included in it.

129
00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:11.210
The overwhelming majority of the deaths came not from the volcano but from the water.

130
00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:15.180
Krakatoa killed people not by burning them but by drowning them.

131
00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:20.430
The months that followed became a quiet continuation of the disaster in their own way.

132
00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:27.600
The eruption had poured millions of cubic metres of pumice into the sea, and pumice is lighter than water; it floats.

133
00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:33.170
Across the Indian Ocean, floating islands of stone formed, kilometres wide.

134
00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.220
Currents carried them west.

135
00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:39.030
For months ships struggled to push through those layers.

136
00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:49.880
And along with those rafts, the remains of the disaster's victims were carried too: nearly a year later, human bones were found among the heaps of pumice washing up on the east coast of Africa.

137
00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:54.970
The people who died in the Sunda Strait had crossed the Indian Ocean from one end to the other.

138
00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:58.780
The western coast of Java could not recover for years.

139
00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:05.630
Salt mud and ash had covered the fertile rice fields; the soil could not be sown for several seasons.

140
00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:08.480
Most of the survivors migrated inland.

141
00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:16.770
The map of the coastline had to be redrawn, because the seafloor had changed, new shallows had formed, and old passages had closed.

142
00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:21.700
The reason this disaster holds a place of its own in history is not only its scale.

143
00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:27.590
Krakatoa is the first great catastrophe that almost the whole world learned about at nearly the same moment.

144
00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:32.520
What made that possible was the copper cable laid along the ocean floor in those years.

145
00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:43.850
By eighteen seventy, Batavia had been connected to the undersea telegraph network: the cable running out of the city reached Singapore, from there across India to the Mediterranean, and from there to London.

146
00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:48.780
A message could travel from Java to London in something like three hours.

147
00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:56.910
For comparison: only eighteen years earlier, when Abraham Lincoln was assassinated, the news had taken two weeks to reach Europe.

148
00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:07.280
A world in which news spread at the speed of a ship had given way to a world in which it spread at the speed of a cable, and that shift was so recent that nobody had yet grasped its consequences.

149
00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:15.410
On the morning of the twenty-seventh of August, the telegraph line at Anyer went dead; naturally, since the town itself had ceased to exist.

150
00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:18.580
But the line in Batavia was working.

151
00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:30.710
Agents in the insurance business, correspondents for the shipping companies, and a local newsman who passed information to the Reuters agency poured the first fragmentary reports out of the strait into the telegraph.

152
00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:34.360
The messages went to Singapore and were distributed from there.

153
00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:40.570
A few hours after the explosion, London knew that something extraordinary had happened in the Sunda Strait.

154
00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:44.380
The next day the European papers carried the story.

155
00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:50.750
Within a few days, even provincial newspapers in the American midwest were publishing detailed reports.

156
00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:53.760
This was a new experience.

157
00:15:53.760 --> 00:16:02.050
A large part of the world's population, for the first time in their lives, followed an event unfolding on a continent that was not their own.

158
00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:07.620
The papers printed fresh detail every day, and readers waited for the next edition.

159
00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:16.550
The thing we take as ordinary today, a disaster on the other side of the world entering our daily agenda, was invented precisely in those days.

160
00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:23.400
That same speed also created an opportunity for science, and the opportunity was seized with surprising rigour.

161
00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:32.010
The first comprehensive work carried out on the ground was done by Rogier Verbeek, a geologist working for the Dutch colonial mining administration.

162
00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:42.860
In the months after the catastrophe, Verbeek covered the region hand's breadth by hand's breadth, measured ash thicknesses, mapped the traces of the waves, gathered the testimony of survivors,

163
00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:46.350
and published a detailed report in eighteen eighty-five.

164
00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:55.280
That report is a turning point for modern volcanology: for the first time, a great eruption was reconstructed from measured rather than estimated data.

165
00:16:55.280 --> 00:17:02.930
Verbeek correctly diagnosed why the island had vanished; he described the emptying of the magma chamber and the mechanism of collapse.

166
00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:10.260
And at the end of his report he placed a prediction that looked bold for its day: one day a new volcano would rise out of the caldera.

167
00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:16.630
In London, meanwhile, the Royal Society set up a special committee to examine the event.

168
00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:22.840
What that committee did amounts to an early and impressive attempt at global data collection in the history of science.

169
00:17:22.840 --> 00:17:34.490
Letters went out to meteorological stations, lighthouses, ships' captains and colonial administrations all over the world: what did you record on and after the twenty-seventh of August?

170
00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:37.340
Would you send us your barograph papers?

171
00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:40.830
Would you describe the colours of your sunsets?

172
00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.520
The answers flowed in for years.

173
00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:55.810
The resulting report, compiled under the direction of the meteorologist Symons, was published in eighteen eighty-eight: hundreds of pages of measurements, testimony, maps and colour plates showing the hues of the sky.

174
00:17:55.810 --> 00:18:03.780
What that report brought to light was an idea nobody had previously held with evidence in hand: the atmosphere is a single system.

175
00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:15.270
Seeing the same small jump appear seven times on barographs around the world, at regular intervals following one another, proved that a pressure wave produced at a single point could travel around the entire planet.

176
00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:26.440
The way the sunset reports spread over the months, first westward along the equator and then north and south, revealed the existence of a continuous and fast-moving wind system in the stratosphere.

177
00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:32.490
The air was not a local phenomenon divided by national borders; it was a single connected covering.

178
00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:36.620
Krakatoa demonstrated this in measurable form for the first time.

179
00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:41.230
Even after the mountain fell silent, the sky went on talking.

180
00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:48.400
The sulphur compounds the eruption had flung into the stratosphere turned into a layer of fine sulphuric acid droplets up there.

181
00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:56.050
Such a layer is not washed out by rain; it hangs for months, even years, and scatters sunlight in unfamiliar ways.

182
00:18:56.050 --> 00:19:01.620
From the autumn of eighteen eighty-three onwards, sunsets became abnormal everywhere in the world.

183
00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:09.430
The horizon was painted in blood-red, purple and copper tones; the sky went on glowing long after darkness had fallen.

184
00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:15.160
People did not know the cause, and their reactions were sometimes comic and sometimes unnerving.

185
00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:24.410
In November eighteen eighty-three, in the northeast of the United States, in cities such as New Haven and Poughkeepsie, fire crews went on alert and turned out:

186
00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:29.020
they took the redness on the horizon for the sign that a neighbouring town was burning.

187
00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:40.190
In those same months the sun itself grew strange; in some regions the sun and the moon appeared blue or green, because the size of the droplets in the atmosphere filtered particular wavelengths selectively.

188
00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:47.360
This episode is thought to have played a part in spreading the expression "blue moon," used for something out of the ordinary.

189
00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:59.810
Some art historians have suggested that the burning orange sky in Edvard Munch's The Scream may also have been fed by the European skies of the post-Krakatoa years; that is an interpretation, not an established finding.

190
00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:04.100
Beneath what could be seen, something else was happening that could be measured.

191
00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:09.190
That thin layer, reflecting part of the sunlight back into space, cooled the planet.

192
00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:16.520
In the year following the eruption, the average summer temperature in the northern hemisphere dropped by about four tenths of a degree.

193
00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:27.370
The effect did not vanish at once; it took close to five years for global average temperatures to return to normal, and over that period drops of up to one point two degrees on average were measured.

194
00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:38.220
The numbers may look small, but a deviation approaching one degree in a planet's average temperature is large enough to shift harvest calendars, monsoon patterns and the severity of winters.

195
00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:44.910
A geological event that took place over a single afternoon bent the world's climate slightly for several years.

196
00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:47.600
Then the Sunda Strait went quiet.

197
00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:51.250
The water above the caldera was deep and still.

198
00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:53.620
The fishermen slowly came back.

199
00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:58.230
That last prediction in Verbeek's report stayed on paper for forty-four years.

200
00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:04.760
On the twenty-ninth of December, nineteen twenty-seven, the sea began to foam where the old island had stood.

201
00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:07.930
Steam and ash burst out of the water.

202
00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:11.740
The caldera in the depths had begun to fill again.

203
00:21:11.740 --> 00:21:17.950
The first ash cone that formed was worn away by the waves, and so was the second, and so was the third.

204
00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:26.400
But the fourth cone, which broke the surface on the eleventh of August, nineteen thirty, managed to harden itself with lava flows and remain standing.

205
00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:31.170
Local people named it Anak Krakatau, the Child of Krakatoa.

206
00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:33.860
The child grew.

207
00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:43.110
Rising several metres a year, it became one of the fastest-growing volcanoes in the world; by twenty eighteen it stood more than three hundred metres above the sea.

208
00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:47.400
Vegetation took hold on it, birds and insects settled.

209
00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:58.250
For biologists it became a laboratory without equal: one of the very few places on earth that offers the chance to watch, from zero, how life returns to a sterilised island.

210
00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:01.740
And it reminded the world what its father had been.

211
00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:12.270
On the evening of the twenty-second of December, twenty eighteen, after months of ongoing eruption, the southwestern flank of Anak Krakatau slid into the sea as a single piece.

212
00:22:12.270 --> 00:22:18.640
This was not an explosion but a landslide; roughly half the body of the island settled into the water.

213
00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:23.890
Because it did not produce a strong enough earthquake, no seismic alarm sounded anywhere.

214
00:22:23.890 --> 00:22:33.780
Waves rode over the holiday towns on both sides of the Sunda Strait, over the hotels along the shore and the coastal villages, without any warning, in the dark of night.

215
00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:36.950
Four hundred and thirty-seven people died.

216
00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:45.400
One hundred and thirty-five years apart, at the same spot, the same mechanism had worked: the weight of a mountain suddenly handed over to water.

217
00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:52.090
The scale of the two cannot be compared; the event of twenty eighteen is a small one beside eighteen eighty-three.

218
00:22:52.090 --> 00:22:54.780
But both said the same thing.

219
00:22:54.780 --> 00:23:04.350
Earthquake-based tsunami warning systems do not see volcanic tsunamis, because what they are looking for is a shaking of the ground, and the sliding of a mountain is silent.

220
00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:13.600
After twenty eighteen, Indonesia began building new systems that monitor movement on volcanic slopes and rely directly on measurements of sea level.

221
00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:23.810
Today, seen from a ship passing through the Sunda Strait, the view is a calm tropical panorama: blue water, green shores, a few small islands on the horizon.

222
00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:32.260
One of those islands is Rakata, its southern side cut away in a sheer cliff, the surviving half of the mountain that was split in two in eighteen eighty-three.

223
00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:37.670
Beside it stands Anak Krakatau, low and grey, its smoke never letting up.

224
00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:44.040
And beneath the water between the two of them, that great pit descending to the seafloor is still there.

225
00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.890
Krakatoa did not disappear.

226
00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:50.380
It only changed shape, and began to wait.

227
00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:53.870
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

228
00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:57.520
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
